rwhisper 0.4.1, vendored with six changes
========================================

Source: https://crates.io/crates/rwhisper/0.4.1 (MIT/Apache-2.0), by Evan Almloff,
part of the Floneum/kalosm project. `src/` is copied unmodified apart from the hunks
below; `src/melfilters.bytes` and `src/melfilters128.bytes` are the crate's own mel
filter banks. Examples from the published tarball were left out; `Cargo.toml` is the
published normalised manifest with its dangling `[[example]]` entries and its "see
Cargo.toml.orig" header removed, dependencies untouched.

Why a copy instead of a version requirement
-------------------------------------------

Upstream builds the decoder prompt as `<|startoftranscript|>` plus the language token,
and then never adds the task token (`TaskType::Unset` in `WhisperInner::transcribe`).
Whisper was trained on `<|startoftranscript|><|lang|><|transcribe|><|notimestamps|>`, so
the model is left guessing what it was asked to do. For English the guess is right often
enough to look harmless; for Russian it is not. Measured on this machine, release build:

| model          | input                      | upstream 0.4.1                        | patched      |
|----------------|----------------------------|---------------------------------------|--------------|
| small          | 10 s of Russian speech     | English prose ("In recent times...")  | Russian text |
| small          | jfk.wav (English)          | correct                               | correct      |
| base           | 10 s of Russian speech     | `" Вната"`                            | Russian text |
| quantized tiny | 10 s of Russian speech     | empty                                 | not retested |

The language token alone does not make the model transcribe rather than translate or
invent. One token in the prompt is the whole difference, and the field is not reachable
from the crate's public API, which is why this is a vendored patch rather than a
workaround in our code.

Bounding decode latency
-----------------------

Whisper's fallback ladder is six temperatures, and each pass can emit up to
`max_target_positions` (448) tokens. On a noisy capture the model can keep emitting
instead of choosing EOT. Measured here on a 1.8 s recording of room noise: the
upstream ladder ran for more than four minutes without finishing and blocked the
bridge's only request slot; a second `/health` could not be served. Clean speech was
unaffected (2-7 s), so this is a noise-path failure rather than general slowness.

The patch keeps the greedy pass and one sampled retry, caps the output at 128 tokens,
and gives both passes a shared 20 s deadline. The same noisy capture now returns in
25 s and normal Russian transcripts are byte-for-byte unchanged.

Language detection and its candidates
--------------------------------------

Upstream cannot detect a language at all: `WhisperBuilder::language` is an
`Option<WhisperLanguage>`, and `None` is turned into English before the model is
built, so a multilingual model only ever transcribes English. The patch keeps
`None` as "detect", runs Whisper's own one-step detector (the decoder's next-token
logits at `<|startoftranscript|>`, argmax over the ~100 `<|xx|>` tokens) on the
first 30 s window, and exposes the result through `Whisper::last_language` so the
bridge can choose a matching voice. It also resets the choice at the start of every
run, otherwise the first utterance's language would condition all the rest.

Unrestricted detection is fine for a file and wrong for a microphone. A capture of
room noise scores highest as Korean or Norwegian often enough to matter: the decode
is then conditioned on that token and produces invented prose in it, which the
answer follows. `WhisperBuilder::with_language_filter` therefore lets the caller
name the codes that can occur - `ru,en,fi,fr` for this household - and the detector
only scores those. It is a hint, not a restriction: a code outside the set is never
produced, but nothing breaks if the model has only one language.

Steering: a prompt and a language hint
--------------------------------------

`Whisper::set_prompt` gives the decoder text it treats as just heard,
Whisper's own prompt mechanism: `<|startofprev|>` and the prompt's tokens (the
last 223) go before `<|startoftranscript|>`, as model input only, never into
the transcript; word timestamps go without it, since they align by position.
`Whisper::set_language_hint` gives one language a head start of 2.5 logits in
detection. Both are read at every utterance through a shared `Steering`.

Measured on three captures from the frame (`small`):

| capture                      | as before                 | hint ru | hint ru + prompt |
|------------------------------|---------------------------|---------|------------------|
| «Рамка» alone, short         | "Ram" (English)           | «Рам!»  | «Рамка.»         |
| «Рамка» alone                | «Рамка.»                  | same    | same             |
| «Рамка, покажи погоду в Эспоо» | «…покажи погоду ВСПУ.»  | same    | «…в Эспоо.»      |

The prompt was «Рамка, покажи погоду в Эспоо. Рамка, какая погода в
Хельсинки?». Over 30 earlier captures of room noise and chatter it produced no
echo of itself; a few English hallucinations on mumbling ("you") became short
Russian ones, and real English stayed English.

